  "body": "The work of #1649 carries a flipped X and a flipped Y into the posted program. A flipped Z is still refused by name, at the post gate and in the post engine (`post::wcs_flip`).\n\nThe maintainer's words (2026-11-17), quoted in #1649: \"the ability to flip axis from a positive to negative is fundamental and should not be blocked.. posting and generation should take it into consideration rather..\"\n\nWhy Z is not carried yet:\n- Tool-length compensation (G43) adds the tool length in the controller's +Z. On a flipped Z, the program's +Z goes into the work.\n- The canned cycles (G81-G89) read the R plane and the retract (G98/G99) in the controller's Z. A mirror changes which side is \"up\".\n- The clearance and retract heights of each script assume that +Z leaves the work.\n\nOpen questions:\n- Is a flipped Z a real machine setup (a part under the spindle face, a tool that points up), or only a WCS on a fixture face? The answer decides if the post mirrors Z or refuses with a clearer reason.\n- Which dialects can state a negative tool-length direction (G43 versus G44)?\n\nDone means: a flipped Z either posts a program that a controller reader with the flipped axis traces on the CAM path (the equivalence test in `crates/post/tests/wcs_flip.rs`), or the refusal names the exact reason and the decision is recorded. The setup sheet, the travel check and the viewport envelope follow.\n",
